From: Koral Ilgun <koral.ilgun@smartrg.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:22:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: answer a repeated FT reassociation after it completed

A non-AP STA can send its Reassociation Request frame again after the
AP completed the FT reassociation, for example when it did not receive
the response. add_associated_sta() keeps the STA entry in this case,
because the TK is configured, and updates the entry with the
association parameters. A driver that tracks the full client state
rejects these parameters for an associated entry, so the AP answered
with status 17 (AP unable to handle new STA).

Skip the update for an FT reassociation when the driver has the entry.
The request passed the FT validation, so it has the nonces and the MIC
of the completed exchange, and the second response has the status and
the content of the first one. The TX status of that response runs the
association callback again, which configures no key. A repeated FILS
association keeps the update and its rejection, because the callback
of a second response would fail the TK configuration and disconnect
the STA.
---
 src/ap/ieee802_11.c |   16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
@@ -6731,6 +6731,22 @@ static int add_associated_sta(struct hos
 		  * once per authentication exchange to guarantee a new TK is
 		  * used here */
 		sta->ft_over_ds = 0;
+	} else if (set && !sta->added_unassoc &&
+		   sta->auth_alg == WLAN_AUTH_FT &&
+		   FULL_AP_CLIENT_STATE_SUPP(hapd->iface->drv_flags)) {
+		struct hostap_sta_driver_data data;
+
+		/*
+		 * The STA completed this reassociation and sent its request
+		 * again, because it did not get the response. The driver entry
+		 * is associated and has the TK. A driver that tracks the full
+		 * client state rejects the association parameters for such an
+		 * entry, so keep the entry as it is. Without an entry in the
+		 * driver, continue to the update that fails, because the TK
+		 * must not go to a new entry.
+		 */
+		if (hostapd_drv_read_sta_data(hapd, &data, sta->addr) == 0)
+			return 0;
 	}
 
 	if (sta->flags & WLAN_STA_HT)
